ust/loopflow/src/bin/lf.rs","rust/loopflow/src/chat/turns.rs","rust/loopflow/src/controller/task/mod.rs","rust/loopflow/src/controller/wave/README.md","rust/loopflow/src/controller/wave/discord.rs","rust/loopflow/src/controller/wave/journal.rs","rust/loopflow/src/controller/wave/mod.rs","rust/loopflow/src/controller/wave/playhead.rs","rust/loopflow/src/controller/wave/recovery.rs","rust/loopflow/src/controller/wave/registry.rs","rust/loopflow/src/controller/wave/resident.rs","rust/loopflow/src/controller/wave/runner.rs","rust/loopflow/src/controller/wave/runtime.rs","rust/loopflow/src/controller/wave/server.rs","rust/loopflow/src/controller/wave/supervisor.rs","rust/loopflow/src/controller/wave/wire.rs","rust/loopflow/src/durable.rs","rust/loopflow/src/engine/builtins/wave/skill/s3-scan.md","rust/loopflow/src/engine/invocation.rs","rust/loopflow/src/engine/mod.rs","rust/loopflow/src/lf/commands/flow.rs","rust/loopflow/src/lf/commands/waves.rs","rust/loopflow/src/lf/mod.rs","rust/loopflow/src/ops/task_execution.rs","rust/loopflow/src/store/migrations.rs","rust/loopflow/src/store/sqlite/durable.rs","rust/loopflow/src/wave_host.rs","rust/loopflow/tests/pr_tests.rs","rust/loopflow/tests/wave_journal_tests.rs","scripts/helpers.sh","scripts/keybindings.sh","scripts/layouts/lf-dev.sh","scripts/layouts/lf-swarm.sh","scripts/loopflow-status.sh","scripts/tmux-review.py","swift/Loopflow/Models/AttemptFailurePresentation.swift","swift/Loopflow/Models/ChatTurn.swift","swift/Loopflow/Services/WaveChatClient.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/LoopflowApp.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Services/Ghostty/GhosttyTerminalView.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Services/ProcessEnvironment.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Services/TmuxSession.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Services/TmuxSessionRegistry.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Views/MessageRow.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Views/RoadmapView.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Views/TaskWorkspaceView.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Views/WaveChatView.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Views/WaveDetailPane.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Views/WavesView.swift","swift/LoopflowMac/Views/WorkSurfaceView.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/AttemptFailurePresentationTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/BundledDaemonPathTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/DTOFixtureTests.swift","swift/LoopflowTests/WaveChatConnectionTests.swift","tests/fixtures/dto/resident_deltas.json","tests/fixtures/dto/resident_door.json","tests/fixtures/dto/wave_attempt_messages.json","tests/fixtures/wave/README.md","tests/fixtures/wave/active-default.jsonl","tests/fixtures/wave/idle-default.jsonl","tests/fixtures/wave/queued-after-skip.jsonl","website/main.py"]},{"sha":"52dbd45589420995b4202e84f8761a91032d44ce","title":"Remove obsolete directions and orphaned .lf memory (#1293)","files":[".lf/directions/task-continuation.md",".lf/name-attribution.md","wave/infrastructure/MEMORY.md","wave/intelligence/MEMORY.md","wave/product/MEMORY.md"]}],"merged_prs":[{"number":1283,"title":"Continue Tasks through review, revision, and saved Flow recovery","body":"Tasks previously stopped after individual autonomous steps, leaving continuation to another launch. This PR drives the saved Flow through successive steps and revision passes, carrying review feedback forward until human input, a blocker, interruption, or Flow completion.\n\n## What changes\n\n- Add `lf task advance` and resumable ordinary Flows, sharing cursor transitions and captured definitions—including nested XOR branches—so recovery preserves the selected work and direction.\n- Make `feature` run design review followed by `pursue`: implementation, compression, behavioral and concept reviews, explicit loop decisions, and an interactive demo. Demo feedback can send work through another implementation pass.\n- Replace human Approve/Iterate controls with **Complete** in the CLI and Sessions UI. Completion returns feedback; the following decision step chooses navigation. Blocked decisions reuse one Ask and reassess its answer.\n- Apply decisions only after their originating Run succeeds, preserve completed feedback across recovery, and reject stale worker settlement.\n- Keep development Session reopen commands on the development executable and stop stored Codex OAuth from overriding native login.\n\nExisting invocations retain their captured definitions; obsolete human navigation policies require a fresh invocation. Finishing a Flow does not implicitly merge its PR or complete its Task. Interrupted external operations still require inspection before retry.\n\n## Checks\n\nRecorded local evidence in [Task continuation evidence](.lf/directions/task-continuation.md) covers continuation, recovery, feedback handoff, Swift Session completion, and Ask fixtures without an installed `lf`, plus formatting and Clippy. Provider/PM effects were simulated. These checks were not rerun for this description; full CI and a live demonstration of the revised demo-to-decision loop remain unverified.\n\n## Try it\n\nSuggested walkthrough, not performed for this description: run `lf task advance <issue>` for a Task with a saved Flow. It should continue to its next stopping point. At a demo Session, record a concrete revision, have the agent mark the feedback ready, then select **Complete**. The following decision should use that feedback to choose another implementation pass or finish the Flow.","files":[],"additions":7861,"deletions":1859,"changed_files":79,"merge_commit":"5bcc40fdff810c39885a31b3fbfd6557f49fde93"},{"number":1285,"title":"build(deps): bump the rust group across 1 directory with 7 updates","body":"Bumps the rust group with 7 updates in the / directory:\n\n| Package | From | To |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| [clap](https://github.com/clap-rs/clap) | `4.6.6` | `4.6.7` |\n| [fancy-regex](https://github.com/fancy-regex/fancy-regex) | `0.19.0` | `0.19.2` |\n| [uuid](https://github.com/uuid-rs/uuid) | `1.24.0` | `1.26.1` |\n| [rand](https://github.com/rust-random/rand) | `0.10.2` | `0.10.3` |\n| [ipnet](https://github.com/krisprice/ipnet) | `2.12.1` | `2.12.2` |\n| [reqwest](https://github.com/seanmonstar/reqwest) | `0.13.4` | `0.13.5` |\n| [tower-http](https://github.com/tower-rs/tower-http) | `0.7.0` | `0.7.1` |\n\n\nUpdates `clap` from 4.6.6 to 4.6.7\n<details>\n<summary>Release notes</summary>\n<p><em>Sourced from <a href=\"https://github.com/clap-rs/clap/releases\">clap's releases</a>.</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<h2>v4.6.7</h2>\n<h2>[4.6.7] - 2026-09-14</h2>\n<h3>Features</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><em>(derive)</em> Add <code>#[command(defer = &lt;bool&gt;)]</code> attribute to opt-in to lazy initialisation of subcommands</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>\n</details>\n<details>\n<summary>Changelog</summary>\n<p><em>Sourced from <a href=\"https://github.com/clap-rs/clap/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md\">clap's changelog</a>.</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<h2>[4.6.7] - 2026-09-14</h2>\n<h3>Features</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><em>(derive)</em> Add <code>#[command(defer = &lt;bool&gt;)]</code> attribute to opt-in to lazy initialisation of subcommands</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>\n</details>\n<details>\n<summary>Commits</summary>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/clap-rs/clap/commit/d3e59a9ab214910b9dad02921b7ef42c6400de9b\"><code>d3e59a9</code></a> chore: Release</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/clap-rs/clap/commit/d997f878c484e02b2935d32a7b67afe990e91227\"><code>d997f87</code></a> docs: Update changelog</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/clap-rs/clap/commit/fb6058cc38cf6f4b07b8db89c850fc80e059e222\"><code>fb6058c</code></a> Merge pull request <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/clap-rs/clap/issues/6409\">#6409</a> from heaths/pwsh-support</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/clap-rs/clap/commit/2310870f7a9fe4c39f5bb5620261a06ac9aaa0b1\"><code>2310870</code></a> test(complete): Add tests for completer_for_path</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/clap-rs/clap/commit/5967c17393a09f90112cdc5b2c20a1383f0bd8e4\"><code>5967c17</code></a> refactor(complete): Move shell detection to Shells</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/clap-rs/clap/commit/594602bb29f374e49da5029df63e5c740c723ad0\"><code>594602b</code></a> fix(complete): Detect pwsh for PowerShell</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/clap-rs/clap/commit/3a4f2d031b5110aaaac40f1cb1d6c0b8ff619df8\"><code>3a4f2d0</code></a> Merge pull request <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/clap-rs/clap/issues/6427\">#6427</a> from clap-rs/renovate/shlex-2.x</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/clap-rs/clap/commit/67ebaed4ac95a83d5d7cc22d64ee0fc162ae1db3\"><code>67ebaed</code></a> Merge pull request <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/clap-rs/clap/issues/6426\">#6426</a> from clap-rs/renovate/actions-checkout-7.x</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/clap-rs/clap/commit/c968b136d0e6f560c08b06a844a20f2798a00096\"><code>c968b13</code></a> chore(deps): Update Rust crate shlex to v2</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/clap-rs/clap/commit/8f247cbf58c582a755ee4dbe62a2390d93b9f8eb\"><code>8f247cb</code></a> chore(deps): Update actions/checkout action to v7</li>\n<li>Additional commits viewable in <a href=\"https://github.com/clap-rs/clap/compare/clap_complete-v4.6.6...clap_complete-v4.6.7\">compare view</a></li>\n</ul>\n</details>\n<br />\n\nUpdates `fancy-regex` from 0.19.0 to 0.19.2\n<details>\n<summary>Release notes</summary>\n<p><em>Sourced from <a href=\"https://github.com/fancy-regex/fancy-regex/releases\">fancy-regex's releases</a>.</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<h2>0.19.2</h2>\n<h3>Added</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Add a <code>leftmost_longest</code> feature (off by default) that adds a method to the <code>RegexBuilder</code>/<code>RegexOptionsBuilder</code> for building a VM which would operate in leftmost-longest match mode instead of the regular leftmost-first mode. Useful for POSIX compliance. (<a href=\"https://","files":[],"additions":22,"deletions":22,"changed_files":1,"merge_commit":"4ba6569f93e3a26d93cbe6f0a52a82e0f8d867f0"},{"number":1287,"title":"Recover blocked PR landings and focus customer prompts","body":"PR landing can now retry a blocked operation and recover on the same commit while showing the repair agent's concrete blocker. Customer prompts also become smaller, with specialized instructions delivered by the skills that use them.\n\n## What changes\n\n- Remove the landing-only launch restriction, lifetime repair ceiling, one-shot incident admission and required SHA change. Reconcile fresh GitHub evidence after every repair; authoritative merge wins over provider errors.\n- Resume blocked landings under the existing generation machinery. Retain the supervisor's operation lock through canceled waiters, preserve active checkout placement, and carry Task disposition through repair publication.\n- Reuse Run final answers for `published`/`blocked` results and CLI repair output. Remove duplicate supervision fields and drop the unused repair counter through a forward migration.\n- Reduce universal operating guidance by 71.8%; move control procedures into their owning skills. Retire stale repository workflows and the obsolete export probe, retain chapter history, and preserve lessons in their existing owners.\n\n## Checks\n\n- 389 affected Rust tests passed, covering engine behavior, landing recovery/takeover, Task settlement, populated migration preservation, prompt goldens and CLI recovery.\n- 75 focused Python checks and two documentation checks passed.\n- Formatting, Clippy (`-D warnings`), architecture ownership, migration history, Python lint and shell checks passed.\n\n## Limits\n\nGitHub and the provider are simulated in local recovery proofs. Live-provider permissions, hosted repair/interruption, lfd HTTP delivery and orphan-provider termination remain unproven. Green-but-unmergeable PRs and the original Etude Task-attribution discrepancy are outside this change. The design's hosted acceptance check remains outstanding.\n\n## Try it\n\nSuggested on an authorized PR: resolve a reported external blocker and rerun `lf pr land` without creating an empty commit. The landing resumes, displays repair conclusions, and finishes when GitHub confirms merge; another concrete blocker is returned with its required action. Preserve the intended Task disposition with `-c` or `--next <slug>`.\n\n\n## CI repair\n\nThe failed head `c8c5c0c7490e4e56172f4620214fd535ba791202` exposed a stale launch-prompt assertion and a terminal test that could time out using its pre-sleep block count. Launch coverage now checks the canonical document; terminal polling reads fresh state before checking its unchanged deadline. TESTING.md records the corresponding local checks.\n\nA temporary four-second wake-up delay reproduced the terminal failure before the fix and passed afterward; the test retains its original 20 ms polling interval. Local verification passed 8 launch tests, 14 builtin tests, 12 terminal tests, Cargo formatting, all-target Clippy with warnings denied, and Swift boundary checks. The full Swift attempt passed the failing terminal case but later stalled and was terminated; it is not counted as a full-suite pass. The repaired head still requires hosted CI.\n\nThe Xcode fallback app and test runners also compiled successfully with `build-for-testing`. Review found no production behavior change or added dependency; prompt content remains owned by the builtin contract tests.","files":[],"additions":1755,"deletions":3009,"changed_files":55,"merge_commit":"9f2e27f05209c0b82156124d2e6a024c02d8c8e2"},{"number":1288,"title":"Preserve people’s names in requests and saved artifacts","body":"Saved decisions could leave readers unsure who requested what, and chat history lacked author names. This PR carries preferred names and request authors into agent context so Tasks, PRs, and summaries can name the right person while conversation still uses “you.”\n\n## What changes\n\n- Add personal `user.name` configuration and `lf name --json`. Carry the caller’s name through launches and SSH; send participant updates on native resume, including clearing a stale name.\n- Preserve request authors through CLI and Mac chat, journal replay, Discord, and Linear. Keep provider identity separate from display names and distinguish requesters from publishers or editors.\n- Update shared guidance, builtin skills, and documentation to use names in saved artifacts while preserving conversational transcripts and historical quotations.\n- Consolidate local chat delivery into one fallible path that journals messages before broadcasting them. Bare interrupts remain independent of name lookup.\n\nUnknown authors remain unknown; names do not establish authorization. Decisions and dated evidence are retained in [.lf/name-attribution.md](.lf/name-attribution.md).\n\n## Checks\n\nRecorded branch verification passed 799 affected Rust tests, 229 Python tests, 78 website tests, 112 focused Swift tests, Rust formatting/clippy, architecture and Swift boundary checks, and the Mac build. Three website viewport checks were skipped. These results were reviewed, not rerun for this description.\n\nThe full Swift package run stalled and was terminated; a complete pass remains unproven. Live native review-session continuation, native rendering, and hosted UI remain unverified.\n\n## Try it\n\nSuggested walkthrough: set `user.name` in personal configuration, then ask a fresh session to write a decision summary and reply conversationally. The summary should use the configured name; the reply should use “you.” Change the preference and repeat—the new summary should use the corrected name without reassigning historical requests.\n\nRecorded fresh CLI generations demonstrated this distinction and preserved unknown attribution. Those were generated examples, not live Task writes.","files":[],"additions":2165,"deletions":882,"changed_files":159,"merge_commit":"9f9f9332fd0cf52adfdb5452f362cc763fe6220c"},{"number":1289,"title":"Recover branch publication and keep learnings in Wave memory","body":"Rebase recreates deleted remote branches despite stale tracking refs, while leases continue protecting unseen remote work. Preparing a replacement PR head now explicitly dequeues queued PRs; GitHub CLI's --disable-auto command returns success without dequeuing them.\n\nFresh landing fixtures use the ephemeral store constructor, and fixture lessons live in infrastructure Wave memory. The memory skills resolve an unnamed Wave from repository objectives and create one repo-wide Wave when none exists. Authoring guidance no longer sends learning notes to .lf/.\n\nChecks: deleted-branch and queued-publication regressions, the authoritative-merge watcher, 14 builtin prompt checks, architecture checks, formatting, and clippy pass. Local transport tests use bare remotes with simulated GitHub/provider responses. Live GitHub branch recreation succeeded. The landing suite passed 19 scenarios; concurrent recompilation invalidated one running executable, and that repeat-landing scenario passed in isolation.","files":[],"additions":327,"deletions":119,"changed_files":12,"merge_commit":"2eaa8c4a96e0a37fa0f756e1a994cf56a65c1f7e"},{"number":1290,"title":"Pair minor releases with closing patches and full-cycle notes","body":"Minor releases now summarize the completed cycle and share their product snapshot with its closing patch. If changes remain, publish the next patch first; otherwise reuse the latest completed patch. Patch notes compare with the preceding release, while minor notes compare with the preceding .0 tag.\n\n- Persist the selected patch/minor pair so retries finish publication without selecting new versions or stopping at the no-change check.\n- Add `lf release notes VERSION --preview` without changing release archives or manifests. Existing versions end at their historical tag and use historical context.\n- Bound PR discovery by the release range and retain coverage across the cycle before trimming source detail.\n\nA minor stops before tagging if the merged candidate differs from its prepared snapshot. Pair recovery uses a local controller receipt; this does not add cross-machine handoff.\n\nChecks: 109 focused release/builtin tests passed for the paired-release implementation; 56 release integration tests passed after historical preview support; seven affected source/preview tests passed after the final query adjustment. Cargo formatting and Clippy with warnings denied pass. Publication tests simulate GitHub/provider boundaries; no release was published.\n\nObserved previews: v0.13.0 since v0.12.0, v0.12.0 since v0.11.0, and v0.11.0 since v0.10.0 completed through the configured notes provider. The two historical previews retained all 187 and 61 commits and matching PRs respectively; original archives remained unchanged.","files":[],"additions":994,"deletions":91,"changed_files":10,"merge_commit":"1549fcc3fe50366d2a38b5eba1cca2a12a7bc234"},{"number":1291,"title":"Run Wave governance as single attempts and remove tmux desktop integration","body":"Waves currently carry a second Flow interpreter and send its cursor to the app just to describe attempts. This change runs one `wave/operate` attempt per wake and keeps failures, input claims and provider sessions in the turn journal, reducing the execution machinery operators and maintainers must understand.\n\n## What changes\n\n- Remove Wave stack traversal, queue/skip controls, the subprocess fallback and playhead HTTP/SSE/client state. Tasks and ordinary Flows retain their execution engines and captured definitions.\n- Preserve historical journals with an explicit cutover disposition. `lf wave recover <name>` displays the original continuation; cancellation requires its exact source sequence and a reason. Unknown provider liveness blocks replacement.\n- Present failures from recorded attempts. Independent scheduled successes cannot erase earlier failures; historical retries retain their attribution.\n- Remove the tmux plugin and desktop tmux shells. Task terminals use Ghostty with a pool owned by each workspace; Session and background-launch behavior is unchanged.\n\nThe diff removes 1,698 net lines outside scratch, including docs and tests. Shared invocation types move to the engine; historical decoding remains read-only.\n\n## Limitations\n\nUnclosed attempts still need authoritative termination reconciliation, and this PR does not add that command or transfer arbitrary old queues into ordinary Flows. Listener and resident must use matching binaries. Configured live-provider/client acceptance remains outstanding; deterministic transport fixtures are simulated evidence.\n\n## Checks\n\n- Rust: 1,988 passed, 6 skipped in the materialized graph with isolated Home; formatting and all-target Clippy passed.\n- Python: 229 passed. Website: 78 passed, 3 skipped. Swift: 267 passed, plus platform boundaries, macOS build and eight distinct fixture renders.\n- Xcode fallback app/test-runner compilation passed, closing the prior resource-blocked verification gap. Architecture and whitespace checks passed.\n\nThe default local gate remains red on an unchanged Flow Session fixture: its private `LF_HOME` conflicts with the materializer's `LF_CONTROL_HOME`. The same compiled test fails with that pin and passes without it; the full Rust pass above removes the pin. `TESTING.md` records the requirement. This gate does not change the unrelated fixture or claim a default-runner pass. Hosted UI acceptance and configured live provider/client execution remain outstanding.\n\n## Try it\n\nSuggested configured acceptance: start a Wave, deliver a Work observation and observe one governance turn followed by idle. Chat should reply independently during governance. Interrupt a running attempt and confirm its reason remains visible after reconnect; a later unrelated successful wake must not label it recovered. In a Task workspace, open two shell tabs, switch between them, and confirm each retains its shell.\n","files":[],"additions":2050,"deletions":3748,"changed_files":72,"merge_commit":"1a691ac6a222b95c46859c9c06d162d6442950a4"},{"number":1293,"title":"Remove obsolete directions and orphaned .lf memory","body":"Remove the retired `.lf/directions/` directory and orphaned `.lf/name-attribution.md`. Curate useful continuation, attribution, and feedback lessons into the existing Wave memories, preserving historical evidence and unresolved acceptance gaps.\n\nChapter archives remain unchanged in `.lf/chapters/`. Active configuration, the telemetry flow, and ignored runtime state remain intact.\n\n## Checks\n\n- Repository architecture scan and 20 architecture tests passed during cleanup.\n- Chapter archive relocation and its associated code/skill edits were reverted; all 1,141 archived files retain their original content.\n- Naming conventions and the prohibition on fallback `.lf/` memory already live in existing guidance.","files":[],"additions":148,"deletions":407,"changed_files":5,"merge_commit":"52dbd45589420995b4202e84f8761a91032d44ce"}],"decisions":null,"previous_release_notes":"# v0.12.21\n\n<!-- loopflow:release-notes=narrative;gate=safe -->\n\nv0.12.21 makes it easier to change plans without losing active work or the evidence behind it. Waves gain resumable chapters, Task status separates execution from lifecycle, and PRs explain the specific change before its Task context. Installation now updates the published release from any directory, independently of source-checkout maintenance.\n\n## Replace the plan, preserve the work\n\nEach Wave now has one current chapter, with Tasks and priorities presented directly through Wave → Task navigation. Chapter transitions preserve work already underway and leave dated history available for review.\n\n- Started, unfinished Tasks retain their identity, worktree, PR, and Flow state across chapters. Untouched backlog is abandoned; uncertain evidence stays unresolved.\n- Chapter previews and durable transition receipts support recovery from interrupted transfers and lost provider responses.\n- Reusable metric instruments belong to the Wave; targets belong to its chapter. Closed chapters preserve readings and evaluation dates, so later Task completion or instrument changes cannot rewrite their history.\n- CLI and desktop surfaces replace ordinary Project selection with chapter and Task views, including explicit unavailable or stale evidence.\n\n## See what a Task is doing and what its PR will deliver\n\nA Task's lifecycle label no longer has to stand in for execution evidence. Status and recommendations now share that evidence, while Task PRs keep the author's specific change at the front of the review.\n\n- Task status includes execution state, reason, current step, worker Run, and recent Run history. Active or uncertain execution suppresses duplicate implementation recommendations; worker evidence takes precedence over dirty files.\n- Runs, Usage, Activity, and Top resolve Work identity consistently, keeping named workers and internal-ID helpers discoverable after renames.\n- PR publication preserves authored titles and opening summaries, followed by one managed Task note describing the merge consequence.\n- Refreshing an armed PR at the same head retains its recorded merge consequence. Publishing a changed head reports that no Task settlement is requested.\n\n## Update Loopflow from any directory\n\n`lf install` now selects and installs a published release without requiring a source checkout or refreshing Git state, packages, and the Python environment. Checkout updates remain the responsibility of `lf rebase`.\n\n- Release downloads use a resolved, pinned tag and installer checksum verification. Complete installations skip asset downloads; missing or stale artifacts trigger repair.\n- Promotion and recovery handle first installation without a previous installed selection.\n- Machine commands and catalog inspection no longer require a repository. Listings retain repository scope inside a checkout and show machine-wide records outside one.\n- Older CLIs retain their `scripts/install.py refresh` upgrade entry point as a delegate to the release installer, preserving install-directory overrides and failure reporting.\n\n## Operational notes\n\n- Chapters change CLI commands, JSON shapes, and metric authoring. Update Rust and Swift consumers together. Rotation changes Linear membership, cancels untouched backlog, and archives predecessor Projects; missing or conflicting evidence can leave reconciliation pending.\n- Prompt directions are removed from flags, configuration, Skill/Flow parsing, discovery, and prompt injection, without a compatibility path. Migrate instructions that depended on that feature. Historical direction traces remain readable.\n- macOS scheduled updates now default to login plus weekly. Rerun `lf install schedule` to adopt the new configuration; positional `daily`, `hourly`, and `5min` options are available. Automatic scheduling remains macOS-only.\n- Task execution describes the Task Flow. An idle state does not establish that independent helpers or interactive Sessions have stopped, and this release adds no launch lock. Recent Task Run history is limited to 50 Runs started within seven days.\n- The repaired legacy installer completed a real published v0.12.20 CLI, daemon, and Mac app installation. That validates the upgrade entry point, not installation of this candidate; populated historical Home migration remains a separate validation requirement.\n\n## Small changes\n\n- Untargeted metric readings retain their values without pass/fail judgments.\n- PR-writing and reporting guidance separates automated evidence from user walkthroughs and uses readable links.","source_limits":{"context_bytes":131072,"commits":256,"merged_prs":200,"files_per_change":100,"area_scopes":32,"name_bytes":256,"title_bytes":300,"path_bytes":512,"pr_body_bytes":4096,"decisions_bytes":16384,"previous_release_notes_bytes":16384,"release_notes_bytes":61440},"omissions":{"commits":0,"merged_prs":0,"commit_files":59,"pr_files":0,"area_scopes":0,"text_bytes":40933,"decisions_bytes":0,"previous_release_notes_bytes":0}}